\section{The character matrix}{

A plain data frame with one row per taxon, the taxon names in a column
(\code{Taxon} by default) and one column per character. Cells are text:
\itemize{
\item a state name, or several separated by \code{sep} when the taxon is variable
(\code{"red/yellow"});
\item for numeric characters a value or range written the way DELTA and floras
write it: \code{"5"}, \code{"2.3-7.9"}, \code{"2.3-5.1-7.9"} (the middle value is the
mean or median) and, with rare extremes, \code{"(1-)2-8(-12)"};
\item for text characters the text;
\item \code{NA} when the character is unknown, not coded or not applicable (give
\code{inapplicable} to tell "not applicable" apart).
}
}

\section{What is read from SDD}{

Categorical, quantitative and text characters, in the order of the
\verb{<Characters>} list; the coded descriptions (\verb{<SummaryData>}) of each
taxon; \verb{<Status>} codes (\code{NotApplicable} becomes \code{inapplicable}, the
others \code{NA}); the dependency rules of the character tree (\code{InapplicableIf},
\code{OnlyApplicableIf}); and observation-convenience ratings, which become a
\code{Cost} from 1 (easy) to 5. A taxon with several descriptions gets one row,
with the states of all of them. States scored with a "misinterpretation"
modifier (Lucid's way of catching a common mistake) are not taken as
states of the taxon, and states scored with the modifier the Lucid Builder
labels "unscoped" make the cell "not applicable" (the Builder defines that
modifier in every SDD file it writes; none of the real exports dimoose
was checked against uses it, so how it is attached to a score is assumed
to be the same as for the other modifiers). Other modifiers (rarely,
uncertain) are ignored and the state kept, so a character whose states
are all "uncertain" for a taxon lists all of them. Dependency rules are
read from the character itself and from the groups of the character tree
it sits in. Quantitative measures map to the range text as follows:
\code{Min}/\code{Max} are the ends of the range, unless \code{UMethLower}/\code{UMethUpper}
are given too, in which case those are the normal range and \code{Min}/\code{Max}
the extremes in parentheses; \code{Mean} (or \code{Median}, \code{Mode}) is the middle
value. Other statistics (\code{SD}, \code{N}) are dropped.

Not read: sample data (\verb{<SampleData>} of individual specimens), media,
natural-language descriptions, taxon hierarchies, and text in more than
one language (the first label of each object is used).
}

\section{What is written}{

A complete, schema-valid SDD 1.1 document with the taxon names, the
characters, a character tree holding the dependency rules, and one coded
description per taxon. \code{NA} cells are left out (SDD reads a missing
coding as unknown), cells equal to \code{inapplicable} get the status
\code{NotApplicable}. A \code{Cost} from 1 to 5 is written as an
observation-convenience rating, as Xper3 does. \code{"ordered"} characters are
written as categorical ones and \code{"integer"} as quantitative ones, so those
two types come back as \code{"categorical"} and \code{"real"}. Of a cell listing
several numeric ranges (\code{"5/10-15"}) the overall range is written, and
numbers are written as numbers (\code{"0.40"} comes back as \code{"0.4"}). A
categorical character for which no taxon has a state is written as a text
character, with a warning, because SDD does not allow a categorical
character without states. Files written from the real datasets dimoose
was tried on (DELTA, Lucid and Xper exports of up to 969 taxa) all
validate against the SDD 1.1 schema; none has been imported into Lucid
or Xper.
}

